    "result_text": "The full build is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, all tests pass). \n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented `capacityWarning` as a read-time-computed boolean warning, following the exact pattern the codebase already uses for `bulkSignupWarning` (the parallel \"warning\" flag), rather than inventing a new mechanism.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`CityCapacityWarningEvaluator`** (new, in `rest.controller` alongside `CityCapacityValidator`) \u2014 a single-responsibility component that returns `true` when the owner's city holds between 40 and 49 owners. It reuses `ClinicService.countOwnersInCity` (the same count the hard-rejection validator uses) and references `CityCapacityValidator.MAX_OWNERS_PER_CITY` so the limit of 50 stays a single source of truth. The warning band is `count >= 40 && count < 50`, so the warning stops exactly where the hard rejection (unchanged) takes over.\n\n2. **`OwnerMapper`** \u2014 autowired the new evaluator and added one `@Mapping` expression for `capacityWarning`, mirroring the existing `bulkSignupWarning` line.\n\n3. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 declared the read-only `capacityWarning` boolean on the Owner schema so the generated `OwnerDto` exposes it in `GET /api/owners/{id}`.\n\nThe hard rejection at 50 in `CityCapacityValidator` was left untouched, as specified. No class became a catch-all \u2014 the reject logic and the warning logic live in separate, focused components that share the same underlying count and constant.",
